import{_ as o,o as t,c as a,a2 as d}from"./chunks/framework.BM5YPQmL.js";const b=JSON.parse('{"title":"Boot flow, stage by stage","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"arch/boot-flow.md","filePath":"arch/boot-flow.md"}'),c={name:"arch/boot-flow.md"};function r(s,e,i,n,l,h){return t(),a("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[d('<h1 id="boot-flow-stage-by-stage" tabindex="-1">Boot flow, stage by stage <a class="header-anchor" href="#boot-flow-stage-by-stage" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Boot flow, stage by stage&quot;">​</a></h1><p>The release ISO boots through <strong>OCBL</strong>, the in-tree boot loader - GRUB left the release chain in 3.0.0-WP05 B5-7 (it survives only as the legacy <code>grub.cfg</code>/<code>grub-ab.cfg</code> disk contract and as the embedded boot-data payload for the in-system <code>install</code>/<code>abdisk</code>/<code>grub-install</code> commands). This page walks the whole chain: stage1 -&gt; OCBL core -&gt; scanner -&gt; menu -&gt; multiboot2 handoff -&gt; kernel entry -&gt; kmain. The component panorama is <a href="./system-map.html">system-map.md</a>; the state monitor&#39;s role is <a href="./hidden-mechanisms.html">hidden-mechanisms.md</a>.</p><h2 id="stage-1-the-512-byte-sector-ocbl-bios-stage1-asm" tabindex="-1">Stage 1 - the 512-byte sector (<code>ocbl/bios/stage1.asm</code>) <a class="header-anchor" href="#stage-1-the-512-byte-sector-ocbl-bios-stage1-asm" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Stage 1 - the 512-byte sector (`ocbl/bios/stage1.asm`)&quot;">​</a></h2><p>One source, two flavors: <code>-D OCBL_ELTORITO</code> (ISO; the whole image is preloaded, core already at 0x7E00, prints <code>OCBL stage1 ok</code>) and <code>-D OCBL_MBR</code> (installed disk; deliberately SILENT - the 512-byte budget follows the GRUB boot.img / ISOLINUX stage1 pattern).</p><ul><li>Entry at 0x7C00; both <code>0000:0x7C00</code> and <code>0x07C0:0x0000</code> entry forms accepted via a <code>call .ip / pop ax</code> self-address check; anything else halts with a serial message.</li><li>Writes <code>{boot drive, flavor, entry CS}</code> to linear 0x520..0x523 for the 64-bit core.</li><li><strong>MBR flavor</strong>: raw-reads the core to 0x7E00 with int 13h AH=42h using the patch block OCBL-install writes at image offset <code>+0x1A0</code>: <code>u32 magic &#39;OCBL&#39;</code>, <code>u32 core_lba_lo</code>, <code>u32 core_lba_hi</code>, <code>u16 core_sectors</code>. The retry loop (12 attempts, ISA pause port 0x1ED) <strong>never resets the drive</strong> - a reset clears SeaBIOS&#39;s PIO-fallback state and loops forever (live-caught WP06b b5).</li><li>A20: int 15h AX=2401 then port 0x92 (bit 0 = fast reset masked off); verification is two independent write patterns (orig^0x55, orig^0xAA) against the 0xFFFF:0x0510 alias - OFF only when BOTH alias tests fail (single-coincidence distrust, the B1 fix). The keyboard-controller method is intentionally absent (declared).</li><li>GDT at 0x7C00 (0x08 code32, 0x10 data32), <code>cr0 |= 1</code>, <code>jmp 0x08:0x7E00</code>.</li><li>Bytes 446..509 are zero in the image - <code>ocbl-install</code> copies the real partition table over them; 510..511 = 0xAA55.</li></ul><h2 id="stage-2-the-ocbl-core-bios-flavor-ocbl-bios-core-head-s" tabindex="-1">Stage 2 - the OCBL core, BIOS flavor (<code>ocbl/bios/core_head.S</code>) <a class="header-anchor" href="#stage-2-the-ocbl-core-bios-flavor-ocbl-bios-core-head-s" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Stage 2 - the OCBL core, BIOS flavor (`ocbl/bios/core_head.S`)&quot;">​</a></h2><p>32-bit entry <code>core_head</code> at 0x8200 (the image links at 0x7E00 via <code>ocbl/linker_bios.ld</code>, <code>.text.head</code> first): flat segments, own GDT (32+64-bit code), zero .bss, identity page tables (PML4@0x8F000 -&gt; PDPT@0x90000 -&gt; PD@0x91000..0x94000, 2 MiB huge pages = 4 GiB), PAE+LME+PG, far jump to 64-bit, stack top 0x8E000 (the WP06f VMware fix - the old 0x9F000 collided with VMware&#39;s larger EBDA). The <strong>EBDA guard</strong> (#134): reads BDA 0x40E; on overlap it relocates the 6-page PT block to 0x40000.</p><p>All firmware services run through the real-mode trampoline (<code>tramp16.asm</code> blob copied to 0x40000; <code>ocbl_bios_int(int_no, regs*)</code> is the 64-bit wrapper - reg block at 0x40300, far jump to selector 0x08 -&gt; 0x40000, resume via saved RIP/RSP). Backends built on it: int 10h VGA text console, int 1Ah clock (54.9 ms ticks), int 16h PS/2 + int 14h serial keys, int 13h disks (AH=48h geometry, AH=42h extended reads, drives 0x80..0x8F + boot CD 0xE0+), int 15h E820 (stored multiboot2-shaped at 0x60000), VBE (mode enumeration + LFB set; ModeInfoBlock offsets live-verified against SeaBIOS), RSDP scan (EBDA then 0xE0000-0xFFFFF, 20/36-byte checksums), and the VBR chainload.</p><h2 id="stage-2-the-ocbl-core-uefi-flavor-ocbl-efi" tabindex="-1">Stage 2&#39; - the OCBL core, UEFI flavor (<code>ocbl/efi/</code>) <a class="header-anchor" href="#stage-2-the-ocbl-core-uefi-flavor-ocbl-efi" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Stage 2&#39; - the OCBL core, UEFI flavor (`ocbl/efi/`)&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Built as an intermediate ELF (<code>ocbl/linker_efi.ld</code>, <code>.</code> = 0x11000 = PE ImageBase 0x10000 + RVA 0x1000 - &quot;every link-time absolute value equals its final virtual address&quot;) and converted by the in-tree <code>ocbl/elf_to_efi.py</code>, which derives the <strong>complete</strong> <code>.reloc</code> directory from <code>ld --emit-relocs</code> (plain <code>ld -m i386pep</code> emitted a partial directory -&gt; OVMF mis-relocation -&gt; the wrong protocol GUID matched -&gt; #UD; documented live-hunt).</p><p><code>efi_main</code> (MS x64 ABI): zero .bss by hand (EDK2 does not guarantee the raw-vs-virtual slack - live-caught <code>g_ndisks</code> = 2.9 billion); reserve the fixed windows with <code>EFI_ALLOCATE_ADDRESS</code> (0x30000/0x10000, 0x50000/0x10000, 0x60000/0x23000 - collision is a FATAL halt because the layout would corrupt firmware data); statemon window reserve (failure only disables the monitor + <code>oc.stmon=off</code>); BlockIo disk init (GUID 964e5b<strong>21</strong> - not the SimpleFileSystem ...22 one-digit trap, live-caught); then the same scan/menu/handoff as BIOS, with GOP instead of VBE, GetMemoryMap -&gt; E820 instead of int 15h, and an ExitBootServices retry loop (3 attempts) before the 64-&gt;32 handoff blob at 0x30000. OVMF&#39;s 5-minute default watchdog is disarmed (<code>set_watchdog_timer(0,...)</code>) before EBS.</p><h2 id="stage-3-the-six-class-scanner-ocbl-common-ocbl-scan-c" tabindex="-1">Stage 3 - the six-class scanner (<code>ocbl/common/ocbl_scan.c</code>) <a class="header-anchor" href="#stage-3-the-six-class-scanner-ocbl-common-ocbl-scan-c" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Stage 3 - the six-class scanner (`ocbl/common/ocbl_scan.c`)&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>ocbl_scan_all()</code> enumerates drives (BIOS int-13 ranges / UEFI BlockIo handles), gates CD-likes to 2048-byte sectors, and per drive runs a partition sweep then a whole-disk &quot;superfloppy&quot; fallback (the OCIRE 16 MiB 0xEE-shadow volume decodes this way when the 0xEE entry itself is the volume). <code>judge_volume()</code> short-circuit priority: <strong>OCSE -&gt; OCOS -&gt; OCIRE -&gt; WINDOWS -&gt; LINUX -&gt; GENERIC</strong>:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Class</th><th>Detection</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>OCSE</td><td>ISO9660 volume carrying <code>/ocse.mrk</code> (the 8.3-safe marker; judged FIRST - an install medium never boots as OCOS even with a kernel on it). Path <code>/boot/ocse.elf</code></td></tr><tr><td>OCOS</td><td>Any volume with <code>/boot/opencube.elf</code> whose first 8 KiB contain mb2 magic <code>0xE85250D6</code>. Marker-less ISOs register too</td></tr><tr><td>OCIRE</td><td>Any volume (incl. the 0xEE shadow and ISOs) with <code>/boot/ocire.elf</code></td></tr><tr><td>WINDOWS</td><td><code>/EFI/Microsoft/Boot/bootmgfw.efi</code></td></tr><tr><td>LINUX</td><td>ESP with <code>/EFI/&lt;vendor&gt;/{grubx64,shimx64,systemd-bootx64}.efi</code> (the vendor dir names the label; <code>BOOT</code> skipped -&gt; falls to GENERIC)</td></tr><tr><td>GENERIC</td><td><code>/EFI/BOOT/BOOTX64.EFI</code></td></tr><tr><td>SHUTDOWN</td><td>Synthesized, always last, can never scroll away</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Declared limit: no ext2/3/4 driver - Linux/Windows are detected via their ESP, never via ext/NTFS roots. A volume carrying BOTH <code>opencube.elf</code> and <code>ocire.elf</code> exposes the OCIRE recovery entry too (WP06g3-4.4 - the short-circuit used to hide it, &quot;postmortem disaster 4&quot;). The read-only volume layer behind the scanner (<code>ocbl/common/ocbl_fs.c</code>) is FAT12/16/32 with LFN read + ISO9660, sharing a 2 KiB sector window at 0x62000 and a 128 KiB FAT cache at 0x62800. Partition traversal (<code>ocbl_part.c</code>) knows MBR, GPT and <strong>SHADOW</strong> (MBR with one type-0xEE entry and NO GPT header - the OCIRE recovery-volume shape; 0xEE does not blindly mean &quot;read the GPT&quot;).</p><h3 id="the-a-b-judgment-chain-native-inside-judge-volume" tabindex="-1">The A/B judgment chain (native, inside <code>judge_volume()</code>) <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-a-b-judgment-chain-native-inside-judge-volume" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The A/B judgment chain (native, inside `judge_volume()`)&quot;">​</a></h3><p>When the first OCOS volume of a disk carries any of <code>/boot/next_B</code>, <code>/boot/bootfail_B</code>, <code>/boot/ok_B</code> (partition 1 = flags volume):</p><ul><li><code>want = (next_b &amp;&amp; !bootfail) || (!next_b &amp;&amp; ok_b) ? 3 : 2</code> - <strong>partition 3 = slot B, partition 2 = slot A</strong>;</li><li><code>next_B &amp;&amp; bootfail_B</code> -&gt; slot A, label suffix <code>&quot; rollback&quot;</code> (<strong>automatic rollback</strong>: a slot-B kernel writes <code>bootfail_B</code> pessimistically at boot and only a fully-confirmed boot removes it);</li><li>the chosen slot volume is re-mounted and the entry&#39;s drive/base_lba/fs re-pointed at it (<code>e-&gt;slot</code> = 1=A / 2=B / 0=plain);</li><li>partitions 2/3 of that disk are &quot;slot-consumed&quot; - they cannot register their own entries.</li></ul><p>The identical flag semantics exist in <code>grub.cfg</code> (ISO medium), <code>grub-ab.cfg</code> (installed disk) and OCBL-native - three implementations, one contract. <a href="./ota-ab.html">ota-ab.md</a> continues the story on the OCOS side.</p><h2 id="stage-4-the-3-key-menu-ocbl-common-ocbl-menu-c" tabindex="-1">Stage 4 - the 3-key menu (<code>ocbl/common/ocbl_menu.c</code>) <a class="header-anchor" href="#stage-4-the-3-key-menu-ocbl-common-ocbl-menu-c" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Stage 4 - the 3-key menu (`ocbl/common/ocbl_menu.c`)&quot;">​</a></h2><p>80x25 text menu: rows 0 title / 1 hint / 2 countdown / 4..22 a scrolling 19-row window. <strong>Exactly three keys are live: Up (0x48), Down (0x50), Enter (0x1C)</strong> - everything else is consumed and ignored (the contract in <code>ocbl_menu.h</code>; there are no hidden keys). The countdown is <code>OCBL_MENU_TIMEOUT_SEC = 10</code>; Up/Down cancel it (&quot;(timer cancelled)&quot;). The Shutdown row is synthesized last and can never scroll away. The menu is fully drivable over <strong>serial</strong> (a complete ANSI mirror of the VGA menu - inverse video, cursor addressing; CSI arrows <code>ESC [ A/B</code>, <code>\\r</code>/<code>\\n</code> Enter) which is how headless automation selects entries (<code>ocbl/menu_drive.py</code> drives QEMU monitor sendkey with a serial <code>MENU-READY</code> poll - PS/2 is THE injection path; CSI bytes via tcp socket arrive too late). Every menu heartbeat kicks the 6300ESB watchdog.</p><p>Before the menu runs, <code>ocbl_run()</code> has already: printed the banner (<code>OCBL_RELEASE</code> + <code>OCBL_BUILD</code>, CPUID brand, stack), run the B2a probe (trampoline + E820 dump + drive sweep + CD PVD at LBA 16 / own boot image at LBA 34), run the <strong>statemon boot check</strong> (may judge the previous boot failed and reset - not return), scanned and dumped all volumes, probed/armed the watchdog 60 s, and consulted <code>ocbl_statemon_route_pending()</code> - a pending BOOT_FAILED record makes the OCIRE entry auto-selected one-shot.</p><h2 id="stage-5-loading-and-the-multiboot2-handoff-ocbl-common-ocbl-load-c" tabindex="-1">Stage 5 - loading and the multiboot2 handoff (<code>ocbl/common/ocbl_load.c</code>) <a class="header-anchor" href="#stage-5-loading-and-the-multiboot2-handoff-ocbl-common-ocbl-load-c" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Stage 5 - loading and the multiboot2 handoff (`ocbl/common/ocbl_load.c`)&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>ocbl_load_elf(v, path, want_base, &amp;size)</code> reads the ELF64 header + up to 32 program headers through the 0x30000 bounce window; every PT_LOAD is copied to its <code>p_paddr</code> and zero-filled to <code>p_memsz</code>; segments outside <code>[want_base, 0x40000000)</code> are refused; the BUG #46 fix made the <code>p_offset</code>/<code>p_filesz</code>/<code>mem_end</code> bounds overflow-safe (the old unchecked forms allowed near-2^64 values to load attacker bytes or zero the whole window). OCOS and OCSE load at 0x100000; OCIRE at 0x1000000. <code>ocbl_load_raw()</code> whole-file copies the &quot;self&quot; module - the raw kernel ELF placed at 0x800000 (the installer payload pattern: the running system can install itself onto other disks from module memory).</p><p><code>ocbl_build_mbi()</code> writes a GRUB-shaped multiboot2 info at <strong>0x50000</strong>: (1) cmdline <code>oc.slot=A|B|ISO</code> plus statemon extras (e.g. <code> oc.hang=N</code> from the <code>/HANGAT</code> probe - see <a href="./hidden-mechanisms.html">hidden-mechanisms.md</a>); (2) bootloader name &quot;OCBL 3.0.0&quot;; (3) module &quot;self&quot; [start,end] for OCOS; (4) meminfo; (6) mmap with 24-byte entries - <strong>the state monitor pages [0x98000,0x9C000) are carved out as E820 reserved</strong> (split-emit below/overlap/above); (8) the 40-byte RGB framebuffer tag; (15/14) the RSDP copy (36 B rev&gt;=2 / 20 B rev1); (0) end.</p><p>Handoff contract (identical to GRUB, so the kernels&#39; <code>boot.S</code> cannot tell the difference): 32-bit protected mode, paging OFF, GDT may be ours, <code>EAX=0x36D76289</code>, <code>EBX=MBI physical</code>, jump to the kernel&#39;s 32-bit entry. BIOS flavor: in-place 64-&gt;32 downgrade. EFI flavor: ExitBootServices, then the handoff blob at 0x30000.</p><h2 id="stage-6-ocos-entry-ocos-kernel" tabindex="-1">Stage 6 - OCOS entry (<code>ocos/kernel/</code>) <a class="header-anchor" href="#stage-6-ocos-entry-ocos-kernel" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Stage 6 - OCOS entry (`ocos/kernel/`)&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>core_boot_entry.S</code> (<code>_start</code>): loads its own GDT, builds boot page tables (PML4 + 4 PDPT entries + 4 PD tables of 2 MiB huge pages = 4 GiB identity map), enables PAE+LME+NXE+PG+WP, far-jumps to <code>long_mode_start</code> (<code>core_boot_longmode.S</code>), which reloads 64-bit data selectors, sets <code>rsp = __boot_stack_top</code> (16 KiB boot stack in .bss) and calls <code>kmain(magic, mbi)</code> - with the mb2 magic kept in <code>edi</code> and the mbi pointer in <code>esi</code> from the handoff. The multiboot2 header (<code>core_boot_mb2header.S</code>, <code>.rodata.mbthdr</code> first in the image) requests an <strong>800x600x32 RGB framebuffer</strong> - OCOS hard-fails without the fb tag (which is why OCBL sets VBE/GOP mode before handoff, and why OCIRE&#39;s kexec hands the tag over verbatim).</p><p><code>arch_multiboot2.c</code> parses the tag list (cmdline, bootloader name, modules - including the &quot;self&quot; module lookup, meminfo, mmap, framebuffer, RSDP tags 14/15) hostile-input hardened: total_size bounds, per-tag minimums, string NUL-termination, truncated-tag rejection (the BUG-0142 chain).</p><h2 id="stage-7-kmain-the-composition-root-core-boot-main-c" tabindex="-1">Stage 7 - kmain, the composition root (<code>core_boot_main.c</code>) <a class="header-anchor" href="#stage-7-kmain-the-composition-root-core-boot-main-c" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Stage 7 - kmain, the composition root (`core_boot_main.c`)&quot;">​</a></h2><p>In boot-log order, with the printed markers:</p><ol><li>COM1 serial 115200 8N1 + serial-encoder tee.</li><li>mb2 magic validation + mbi parse (fail -&gt; serial <code>FATAL:</code> + hlt).</li><li><code>boot_state_early_init(cmdline)</code> - the state monitor takes over the 6300ESB watchdog, writes stage 1 <code>KM_AIN_ENTERED</code>.</li><li><code>screen_fb_init</code> (RGB 32bpp only; masks from the tag handle BGRX BIOS-VBE and RGBX UEFI-GOP) -&gt; console -&gt; CJK renderer -&gt; <code>lib_log_init</code> -&gt; <code>klog_init</code>. &quot;boot chain 1/4: kernel core mechanisms&quot;.</li><li>IDT/GDT/TSS (256 gates), 8259 remap, PIT 100 Hz, PS/2 keyboard, COM1 RX -&gt; key queue, line editor, <code>sti</code>, exception self-test (#DE/#UD/#PF caught, kernel alive), PMM/VMM/heap, <code>klog_grow()</code> (the 24-entry bootstrap ring becomes the 1024-entry heap ring - nothing is lost).</li><li>scheduler + sync + usermode; <strong>registry collect</strong> (<code>drv/svc/core_registry</code> - logs <code>registry: drv=N svc=N app=N</code>); ACPI/AML/PCIe/watchdog-probe/i2c/RTC/HPET. &quot;boot chain 2/4: drivers + driver-free services&quot;, stage 2 <code>SCHED_READY</code>.</li><li>VFS + ramfs (+ klog dump to /var/log/kernel.log), block cache + probe, SCSI, USB probe + resident poll thread; FAT32/exFAT/ext4 registries, config, PM timer, A/B slots, wireless/SDIO/Bluetooth, camera/legacy ports, LAPIC, net stack + 10 ms timer, health sampler, audio probe. &quot;boot chain 3/4: driver-dependent services&quot;, stage 3 <code>DRIVERS_READY</code>.</li><li>Crash infrastructure armed (<code>core_dump_alloc</code> once up-front, panic handlers, crashlog, soft watchdog - <a href="./crash-recovery.html">crash-recovery.md</a>); optional async update check; <code>ota_update_confirm_boot()</code> (clears the slot-B bootfail marker - the other end of the A/B chain); <code>ocembed_boot_confirm()</code> on supervised boots (mailbox SIGNAL_OK, i6300esb disarm, whole-disk write protect, ocire_guard spawn). &quot;boot chain 4/4: interfaces ready&quot;, stage 4 <code>SERVICES_READY</code>.</li><li>&quot;L0 ready, waiting for L1&quot; -&gt; <code>l1_launcher_boot_default()</code> spawns the ring-3 <strong>ush</strong> shell (the default <code>OC_L1_APP_DECL</code> entry in <code>core_boot_l1launch.c</code>), marks stage 5 <code>L1_LAUNCHED</code>, and parks the boot task in a yield loop; kmain ends in <code>for(;;) hlt</code>. The FIRST syscall from ush flips stage 6 <code>L1_READY</code>.</li></ol><p>The scanner test fleet (<code>ocbl/make_scan_disks.py</code>) locks this chain down: hd0 (MBR 3-part A/B FAT32 OCOS flags volume -&gt; slot B entry), hd1 (0xEE shadow FAT12 OCIRE), hd2 (Windows ESP LFN), hd3 (ubuntu ESP), hd4 (generic ESP), fat0 (superfloppy FAT16), bare0 (garbage -&gt; 0 entries), cd0.iso (per-content). The chainload fleet adds chain0 (an ESP whose BOOTX64.EFI is OCBL itself - the self-chainload proof) and chain1 (a non-PE stub that must be refused with EFI_LOAD_ERROR).</p><p>For the user-facing side of the same story: <a href="./../manual/boot-modes.html">../manual/boot-modes.md</a>; for OCSE&#39;s own 24-stage ladder and OCIRE&#39;s supervisor decision loop: <a href="./system-map.html">system-map.md</a> and <a href="./../dev/ocire.html">../dev/ocire.md</a>.</p>',34)])])}const g=o(c,[["render",r]]);export{b as __pageData,g as default};
